From e52b304ebfd32ebc194d9d12e238a2af79d01dda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 23:52:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] opcodes: microblaze: disassemble the word 0

objdump stops disassembling a MicroBlaze section at the first word 0
it meets.  For example, ".align 3" after one instruction pads .text with
a zero word, and objdump -d shows only the first instruction:

	   0:	00642800 	add	r3, r4, r5
	   4:

read_insn_microblaze returns the instruction word, and returns 0 when
the read fails.  print_insn_microblaze cannot tell the two apart:

	inst = read_insn_microblaze (memaddr, info, &op);
	if (inst == 0)
	  return -1;

and objdump gives up on the section when the disassembler returns -1.
The read of the previous word, which looks for an imm prefix, has the
same test.

The word 0 is "add r0, r0, r0".  UG984 says it is an illegal opcode
only when the core is built with C_OPCODE_0x0_ILLEGAL, and QEMU
executes it as add unless the opcode-0x0-illegal property is set.

This makes read_insn_microblaze return false when the read fails and
pass the word back through a new argument, and changes both callers to
test that.  A failed read still reports the memory error and returns
-1, as before.  The two checks have been there since the port was
added in 7ba29e2a41a.

The test the last patch of this series adds ends with "add r0, r0, r0"
and one instruction after it.  Without this change the dump stops at
the zero word and the test fails; with it, it passes.

Tested on microblaze-elf. gas stays at 332 expected passes and 10
unsupported, binutils at 245 expected passes, 2 expected failures, 18
untested and 17 unsupported, and ld at 480 expected passes, 15 expected
failures, 28 untested and 236 unsupported.  No unexpected failures.  GDB
from master stops at the zero word too, with "unknown disassembler
error"; with the whole series applied it goes on.

opcodes/
	* microblaze-dis.c (read_insn_microblaze): Return a bool and
	store the instruction through a new INSTP argument.
	(print_insn_microblaze): Adjust.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 opcodes/microblaze-dis.c | 17 ++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c b/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c
index 6e0957c029f..3158ef74fb0 100644
--- a/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c
+++ b/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c
@@ -197,9 +197,13 @@ get_field_special (struct string_buf *buf, long instr,
    return p;
 }
 
-static unsigned long
+/* Read the instruction at MEMADDR into *INSTP and find its table entry.
+   Return false if the memory cannot be read.  */
+
+static bool
 read_insn_microblaze (bfd_vma memaddr,
 		      struct disassemble_info *info,
+		      unsigned long *instp,
 		      const struct op_code_struct **opr)
 {
   unsigned char       ibytes[4];
@@ -212,7 +216,7 @@ read_insn_microblaze (bfd_vma memaddr,
   if (status != 0)
     {
       info->memory_error_func (status, memaddr, info);
-      return 0;
+      return false;
     }
 
   if (info->endian == BFD_ENDIAN_BIG)
@@ -230,7 +234,8 @@ read_insn_microblaze (bfd_vma memaddr,
       break;
 
   *opr = op;
-  return inst;
+  *instp = inst;
+  return true;
 }
 
 
@@ -251,16 +256,14 @@ print_insn_microblaze (bfd_vma memaddr, struct disassemble_info * info)
   buf.which = 0;
   info->bytes_per_chunk = 4;
 
-  inst = read_insn_microblaze (memaddr, info, &op);
-  if (inst == 0)
+  if (!read_insn_microblaze (memaddr, info, &inst, &op))
     return -1;
 
   if (prev_insn_vma == curr_insn_vma)
     {
       if (memaddr-(info->bytes_per_chunk) == prev_insn_addr)
 	{
-	  prev_inst = read_insn_microblaze (prev_insn_addr, info, &pop);
-	  if (prev_inst == 0)
+	  if (!read_insn_microblaze (prev_insn_addr, info, &prev_inst, &pop))
 	    return -1;
 	  if (pop->instr == imm)
 	    {
